r each paper. USA Today has NewsID 1865 and city_mclean. NYT 292, WSJ 293, WaPo 88. Correct.\n\nNow five cities fact. Code output table of six newspapers' white staff share and city white 2015. Claim: \"In 2017 the white share of the population was 32.2% in Chicago, 9.1% in Detroit, 25.5% in Houston, 29.4% in Dallas and 35.8% in Philadelphia, while white staff were 80.8% at the Chicago Tribune, 75.0% at the Detroit Free Press, 74.4% at the Detroit News, 77.4% at the Houston Chronicle, 77.6% at the Dallas Morning News and 82.1% at the Philadelphia Inquirer.\" This is long but covers all items. Source exact. Code computes for six rows. Need merge map_data 2017 with newsidsunique and census.\n\nPotential issue: The story says \"white populations ... are all less than 40% ... white journalists exceed 74%\". The claim with exact numbers is fine.\n\nNow Desert Sun fact. Code computes Desert Sun 2017 percentages and Palm Springs census. Claim: \"The Desert Sun's staff was 20.6% Hispanic and 11.8% Black in 2017, against 25.8% Hispanic and 4.7% Black in Palm Springs.\" Source exact. This satisfies \"just shy\" and \"more than double\". Should we include \"city's 5%\" rounding? Printout gives 4.7. The story says 5%. Up to rounding. Fine.\n\nBut wait: \"Hispanic staff is just shy of matching Palm Spring's Hispanic population at 26%\" Could be interpreted as Hispanic staff 21% vs 26% (yes). Our claim says 20.6% vs 25.8%. Good.\n\nNow average fact. Need decide. Let's explore story chart image more. Maybe the chart axis is \"% White\" and shows each newsroom. The text \"On average, American newsrooms are still more white...\" No specific number in text. A chart label may show \"Average\" but OCR didn't capture. The static image OCR only got title/axis/names. If the chart shows bars, no numeric labels. We can still create a fact with computed average. But is it safe? The task wants facts the files reproduce. The data clearly reproduces \"newsrooms more white than the city\" for all but 4 newsrooms. We could instead formulate a pattern: \"Of 100 newsrooms with more than 25 staff, 96 have a higher white share than their city's white population.\" But the story says \"On average ... still more white\", not \"almost all\". Using 96/100 is a computed pattern but not exactly the story's claim. The claim should be the finding in one sentence with numbers the code prints. We can say \"Across the 100 newsrooms with more than 25 staff that match a census city, the mean white staff share (84.9%) is 30.1 points above the mean white share of the cities they serve (54.7%).\" This directly reproduces \"on average more white\".\n\nBut matter of filter: story says among newsrooms with over 25 staff, larger geography used when appropriate. Our filter only city-matched. The story's default city and \"larger geography when appropriate\" is for 105 newsrooms; we cannot identify which. But using only city matches is a defensible subset? The instruction: \"a range, threshold or subset is one the story uses, never one chosen to make a number come out right.\" The subset \"city geography\" is one the story uses (default). Excluding unmatched due to data limitation is not choosing to make it right. Fine.\n\nYet there's a subtlety: the 2017 newsroom counts in our file for some large papers may be wrong (USA Today total 274, but earlier 864 in 2015). Fine, 2017 survey.\n\nAlso \"average\" of city white populations unweighted vs weighted. The story's chart might show each newsroom vs its city and an average line. The average line could be mean of newsroom white % and mean of city white %. We'll use unweighted mean. Could also show weighted, but claim only one. Which is more likely? The Pudding interactive \"Average 59% Male\" is average of newsroom percentages (unweighted). For race, likely average of white percentages. Use unweighted.\n\nLet's compute mean with dedup and verify. 84.86 vs 54.73. Maybe filter uses >=25 not >25; 2017 >=25 has 115, a few 25. Story says \"more than 25\", so >25.\n\nPotential issue: Census white_2015 percentages are for city, but story used Census 2011-2015. Good.\n\nNow the chart \"closest to racial parity\": OCR lists Minneapolis Star Tribune, Naples Daily News, The Desert Sun. Our computed gaps (white staff - city white) bottom three are Newsday -8.8, Naples -7.4, Desert Sun -5.5. Minneapolis 81.97 - 60.3 = +21.67, not close to parity. Why chart says closest? Maybe the measure is a composite across all races, not white share only. For Minneapolis: city white 60.3, staff white 82.0 — a 21.7 point gap, far from parity. Yet it's listed as closest. So their \"racial parity\" measure may be a diversity index that weights all groups; or maybe the chart compares each newsroom to a larger geography (Minneapolis Star Tribune to Minnesota, where white population ~85%), making parity close. Ah yes! \"Among newsrooms with over 25 staff members, a larger geography was used when appropriate.\" Minneapolis Star Tribune likely compared to Minnesota (white ~84%), not Minneapolis. Similarly Naples Daily News compared to Collier County (white ~84%?), not city (88.8? actually city 88.8). Desert Sun compared to Palm Springs city. Story text says for Desert Sun \"When comparing the newsroom to the city of Palm Springs (though other geographies, such as neighboring counties could suit as well)\". So chart bars may use arbitrary geography choices; we don't have county/state census, only city. Therefore chart's closest/top gap rankings are not reproducible with our data (except maybe city-only). We should skip those chart labels as findings? They are chart labels (names), but not numbers. The findings from the chart: \"Minneapolis Star Tribune, Naples Daily News, The Desert Sun are newsroom staffs closest to racial parity compared to their home city.\" Our data cannot reproduce because census not matching chosen geographies. We could skip with reason.\n\nSimilarly \"LARGE NEWSROOM STAFFS FURTHEST FROM RACIAL PARITY\" chart names six papers; with city-only gaps, not the same set/order. Could skip.\n\nBut maybe the chart uses a different \"parity\" metric using all race categories and city data. Let's investigate. For Minneapolis, white gap 21.7, but perhaps Black staff vs city, Hispanic vs city, Asian vs city all close? City Minneapolis: white 60.3, black 18.0, hispanic 10.0, asian 6.0. Staff: white 82.0, black 6.9, hisp 4.7, asian 5.6. Sum of absolute differences across categories: 21.7+11.1+5.3+0.4 = 38.5 points. That's large, not close. If composite, no.\n\nSo likely geography differs. The chart's \"closest to parity\" names are not reproducible from city census only. But the story text's exact Palm Springs comparison is reproducible.\n\nThe chart \"furthest from parity\" names includes Chicago, Detroit, Houston, Dallas, Philadelphia — these all do have large city gaps. Reading Eagle etc. are small newsrooms; chart title \"large newsroom staffs\", maybe threshold >100 employees. Let's test >100 excluding national and excluding papers not in chart. Computed top gaps >100:\nWSJ 48.63, NYT 48.52 (national, separate chart)\nDetroit FP 65.9, Detroit News 65.28, Houston 51.87, Chicago 48.64, Dallas 48.23, San Jose 47.54, Philadelphia 46.27, Milwaukee 51.7? Wait >100 filter includes Milwaukee 51.7! Let me recompute proper >100 sorted:\nDetroit FP 65.9\nDetroit News 65.28\nHouston 51.87\nMilwaukee Journal Sentinel 51.70 (total 100, exactly maybe >100 false if strict)\nChicago 48.64\nWSJ 48.63\nNYT 48.52\nDallas 48.23\nSan Jose 47.54\nPhiladelphia 46.27\nCleveland Plain Dealer? total 85 no\nSo chart's list omits San Jose, Milwaukee, and includes Philadelphia. Maybe threshold >150 or >200:\n>150: Detroit FP no (100), Detroit News no. So no.\n\nMaybe \"large newsroom staffs\" is not by total staff but by circulation (Circ A/B?) or market size. Detroit Free Press/News are large circulation papers (Circ A/B). The `Circ` column has categories A, B, etc. Maybe \"large\" means circulation class A/B with >100 staff? Chart names all Circ A/B. Reading Eagle etc. Circ D. The chart may filter newspapers with circulation A or B (major metros). Let's check. Six chart papers:\nChicago Tribune Circ A\nDetroit Free Press A\nDetroit News B\nHouston Chronicle B\nDallas Morning News B (but newsidsunique has three IDs; 427 Circ B, 2114 E, 2371 E)\nPhiladelphia Inquirer A\nAll A/B. San Jose Mercury News maybe Circ A? Let's check. Milwaukee Journal Sentinel maybe A? If so still.\nRead the `Circ` values.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_aMx5QLLRfc40ylWckU8v0021","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/user/RL_Study/report-gepa/runs/originals/asne && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nm=pd.read_csv('data/map_data_3.csv'); n=pd.read_csv('data/newsidsunique_4.csv'); c=pd.read_csv('data/census_4.csv')\nm=m[(m.total_num>0)&m.white_num.notna()]\nt=m[m.Year==2017].merge(n.drop_duplicates('NewsID'),on='NewsID')\nt['city_state']=(t.City.str.lower().str.strip()+' '+t.State.str.lower().str.strip())\nc2=c.copy(); c2['city_state']=c2.city_state.str.lower().str.strip()\nx=t.merge(c2,on='city_state')\nz=x[x.total_num>25].copy(); z['white_staff']=100*z.white_num/z.total_num; z['gap']=z.white_staff-z.white_2015\nprint(z.Circ.value_counts())\nfor circ in ['A','B','C','D','E','F']:\n    print('\\nCIRC',circ)\n    print(z[z.Circ==circ].sort_values('gap',ascending=False)[['Company','city_state','Circ','total_num','white_staff','white_2015','gap']].head(12).to_string())\nprint('\\nall A/B sorted')\nprint(z[z.Circ.isin(['A','B'])].sort_values('gap',ascending=False)[['Company','city_state','Circ','total_num','white_staff','white_2015','gap']].to_string())\nEOF"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-flash","usage":{"input":223,"output":3022,"cacheRead":57088,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":60333,"cost":{"input":0.00003122,"output":0.0008461600000000001,"cacheRead":0.0001598464,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0010