{"type":"message","id":"24c724d1","parentId":"c854bed5","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:07:55.024Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Drop duplicate keep first yields valid 65. Good. 1180 is Asbury Park Press, unmatched city, so doesn't affect >25 city facts. But affects pivot if not deduped; we'll dedupe.\n\nNow let's write the four/five facts, evaluate.\n\nLet's first write candidate facts in a Python script manually to test. Use JSON code strings.\n\nFacts list:\n1. `newsrooms-more-white-on-average`\nsource: \"On average, American newsrooms are still more white than the city, county, or state that they serve.\"\nclaim: \"Across the 100 newsrooms with more than 25 staff that match a city in the census, mean white staff share is 84.9%, against a mean white population share of 54.7% in the cities they serve.\"\ntables: m: data/map_data_3.csv, n: data/newsidsunique_4.csv, c: data/census_4.csv\ncode: ...\nQuestion: \"every file is read with pd.read_csv(path)\"—the evaluator will read our tables variables. Code references m, n, c. Fine.\n\n2. `five-cities-white-gap`\nsource exact sentence: \"For instance, the white populations in Chicago, Detroit, Houston, Dallas, and Philadelphia are all less than 40% of each city’s total population. But, in the newsrooms that cater to those cities, white journalists exceed 74% of the total team.\"\nClaim with all numbers.\nCode merges 2017, >25, five cities, prints Company, city_state, white_staff, white_2015.\nNote the exact quote uses curly apostrophe \"city’s\". We should copy exactly. `source` can include the full two sentences. The instruction says source: the story's own words for this finding, quoted exactly. Good.\n\n3. `desert-sun-parity`\nsource exact: \"When comparing the newsroom to the city of Palm Springs (though other geographies, such as neighboring counties could suit as well), we observe that Hispanic staff is just shy of matching Palm Spring’s Hispanic population at 26%, as well as Black staff reflecting more than double the city’s 5% of the population.\"\nClaim: \"In 2017 the Desert Sun's staff was 20.6% Hispanic and 11.8% Black, against 25.8% Hispanic and 4.7% Black in Palm Springs.\"\nCode prints those.\n\n4. `newsrooms-more-diverse-share`\nsource: \"Over the past 16 years, about 45% of newsrooms became more racially diverse.\"\nClaim: \"Between 2001 and 2017, 44.5% of the 402 newsrooms with valid data in both years increased their non-white staff share.\"\nCode prints count 402 and mean 44.53. Need ensure \"non-white staff share\" is the measure; story doesn't say non-white but okay.\nCould claim \"45%\" rounded, but printout 44.53; claim should say 44.5. Maybe \"about 45%\" acceptable. Use 44.5.\n\n5. `newsrooms-not-more-diverse-share`\nsource: \"However, just over half of the surveyed newsrooms either have maintained their 2001 levels of racial diversity or have become less diverse.\"\nClaim: \"55.5% of the same 402 newsrooms had a non-white staff share in 2017 that was equal to or lower than in 2001.\"\nCode prints 402 and 55.47.\n\n6. `national-papers-wapo-only`\nsource: \"The Washington Post, USA Today, the New York Times, and the Wall Street Journal are all national publications. Of these four, only the Washington Post substantially increased its racial diversity among staff over the past 16 years.\"\nClaim with values.\nCode prints pivot.\n\nShould we add a fact for the overall statement \"This disparity is most extreme in large, racially diverse cities.\"? It's qualitative but supported by five cities. Already included in fact 2. Don't duplicate.\n\nShould we add \"Overall, most newsrooms have some catching up to do if they want to meet the 2025 goal.\" This follows from 55% no progress and four papers; not a distinct quantitative finding. Could skip.\n\nShould we add \"The Desert Sun has nearly reached ASNE's 2025 goals, eight years ahead of schedule\" as separate from specific percentages? The specific percentages show close-ish but not \"nearly\". Code cannot verify \"2025 goals\" without definition. Skip or include in skipped.\n\nSkipped:\n- \"This disparity is most extreme in large, racially diverse cities.\" Maybe not skipped, covered by five-city fact.\n- \"Alternatively, the Desert Sun, a newspaper in Palm Springs, CA, has nearly reached ASNE’s 2025 goals, eight years ahead of schedule.\" Why: the 2025 parity goal definition/geography not in data; only specific staff/city shares reproduced.\n- \"This publication is one of the many newsrooms closest to achieving racial parity in the US.\" Why: ranking depends on hand-chosen larger geographies not in city census.\n- Chart: \"LARGE NEWSROOM STAFFS FURTHEST FROM RACIAL PARITY COMPARED TO THEIR HOME CITY\" names six papers. Why: chart's large-newsroom selection and larger geographies not in data; city-only gaps rank other papers above them.\n- Chart: \"NEWSROOM STAFFS CLOSEST TO RACIAL PARITY COMPARED TO THEIR HOME CITY\" names three. Why: same.\n- \"CHANGES IN NATIONAL NEWSROOM RACIAL DIVERSITY 2001 - 2017 | COMPARED TO THE US POPULATION\" plus/minus axis? The four papers covered. Maybe not skip.\n- \"Although ASNE and newsrooms nationwide did not reach racial parity by 2000...\" Why: audience parity in 2000 requires census_2000 only white, not full race; census has white_2000 but no nonwhite by group for 2000. Could compute white share of newsrooms 2000 vs white_2000 city. But story says \"did not quite reach parity\" — data can show 2000 newsroom white share (average) > city white. But no specific number. Could include? Let's explore. 2000 newsroom white share is available (all race counts 0 except white, but white share valid). Census white_2000. For each 2000 newsroom matched to city, average white staff ~? Story historical claim. But \"racial parity\" goal by 2000 likely about nonwhite share. We can compute. But is it a finding? Yes: \"they didn't quite reach parity by the new millennium\". Could be reproduced: in 2000, newsrooms were more white than their cities. Let's compute mean.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_o40mbVf3hrO5e8CjE8Zt7224","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/user/RL_Study/report-gepa/runs/originals/asne && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nm=pd.read_csv('data/map_data_3.csv'); n=pd.read_csv('data/newsidsunique_4.csv'); c=pd.read_csv('data/census_4.csv')\nfor yr in [2000,2001]:\n t=m[(m.Year==yr)&(m.total_num>0)&m.white_num.notna()].drop_duplicates(['NewsID','Year']).merge(n.drop_duplicates('NewsID'),on='NewsID',how='left')\n t['city_state']=(t.City.str.lower().str.strip()+' '+t.State.str.lower().str.strip())\n c2=c.copy(); c2['city_state']=c2.city_state.str.lower().str.strip()\n x=t.merge(c2,on='city_state')\n z=x[x.total_num>25].copy(); z['ws']=100*z.white_num/z.total_num\n print(yr,'n',len(z),'mean staff',z.ws.mean(),'mean city white2015',z.white_2015.mean(),'mean city white2000',z.white_2000.mean(),'share>',(z.ws>z.white_2000).mean()*100)\nEOF"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-flash","usage":{"input":214,"output":1774,"cacheRead":73600,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":75588,"cost":{"input":0.00002996,"output":0.0004967200000000001,"cacheRead":0.00020607999999999998,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00073276}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1793221665728,"responseId":"94020e67-fc1a-4013-b232-c86adedb2670"}}
