      77.625571        29.4\n73           the detroit news       detroit mi        74.380165         9.1\n74         detroit free press       detroit mi        75.000000         9.1\n39          houston chronicle       houston tx        77.372263        25.5\n51  the philadelphia inquirer  philadelphia pa        82.071713        35.8\n\n--- desert-sun-hispanic-black\nSOURCE: When comparing the newsroom to the city of Palm Springs (though other geographies, such as neighboring counties could suit as well), we observe that Hispanic staff is just shy of matching Palm Spring’s Hispanic population at 26%, as well as Black staff reflecting more than double the city’s 5% of the population.\nCLAIM: In 2017 the Desert Sun's staff was 20.6% Hispanic and 11.8% Black, against 25.8% Hispanic and 4.7% Black in Palm Springs.\nCompany             the desert sun\nhispanic_staff_pct       20.588235\nblack_staff_pct          11.764706\nhispanic_2015            25.800000\nblack_2015                4.700000\n\n--- newsrooms-more-diverse-share\nSOURCE: Over the past 16 years, about 45% of newsrooms became more racially diverse.\nCLAIM: From 2001 to 2017, 44.8% of the 402 newsrooms with data in both years increased their non-white staff share.\nsize    402.000000\nmean     44.776119\ndtype: float64\n\n--- newsrooms-same-or-less-diverse-share\nSOURCE: However, just over half of the surveyed newsrooms either have maintained their 2001 levels of racial diversity or have become less diverse.\nCLAIM: 55.2% of those 402 newsrooms had a 2017 non-white staff share equal to or below their 2001 share.\nsize    402.000000\nmean     55.223881\ndtype: float64\n\n--- national-papers-wapo-only\nSOURCE: The Washington Post, USA Today, the New York Times, and the Wall Street Journal are all national publications. Of these four, only the Washington Post substantially increased its racial diversity among staff over the past 16 years.\nCLAIM: The Washington Post's non-white staff share rose from 19.5% in 200